<h1>Authoring scenes &amp; HUDs</h1>
<p><strong>Authoring</strong> is the act of composing what the game contains: which objects exist in a level, how they are arranged, and what the player sees on screen. In the DemiEngine editor, authoring happens in two integrated stages — scene documents for game worlds and HUD documents for interfaces — with one shared selection model, one undo history per document, and saves that keep your JSON diffs small. This page walks the workflow on the sample game "Skyforge" at <code>games/my_game</code>.</p>

<h2>Switching documents</h2>
<p>The workspace registers the project's scenes and HUDs from <code>demi.project.json</code> and the conventional source folders. Opening a document is one click:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Click a registered <strong>scene source</strong> — the active stage switches to that scene. The hierarchy, viewport, Inspector, diagnostics, and undo history all target the scene document.</li>
  <li>Click a <strong>HUD source</strong> — the HUD stage opens with the same hierarchy, viewport, selection, Inspector, undo/redo, save, diagnostics, and recovery paths as the scene stage.</li>
  <li>Click a <strong>prefab source</strong> — the prefab stage opens for that reusable entity composition.</li>
</ul>
<p>There is no second, modal interface editor and no separate HUD preview model: one workspace, one editing contract, three document kinds. Document shapes are described in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'project-json'}) }}">demi.project.json</a>.</p>

<h2>Create and finish a project in the editor</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Use <strong>File → New project</strong> to choose a starter. <strong>File → New document</strong> (also under <strong>Assets → Create</strong>) creates a 2D/3D scene, HUD, entity or UI prefab, or Lua behaviour. The Assets Create menu also offers materials and data assets.</li>
  <li>Build the scene and HUD in their stages. Use <strong>Scene → Create prefab from selection</strong> to create a reusable source and replace the selection with a linked instance. <strong>Replace selection with a prefab instance</strong> is on by default: entity IDs, placement, hierarchy and references stay intact. Uncheck it to create an unlinked copy. One Undo restores the original hierarchy; the reusable prefab file remains. Add it through <strong>Scene → Add prefab instance</strong>, the Assets action, or a drag onto the Scene viewport.</li>
  <li>Attach a HUD with <strong>Scene → HUD</strong>. Set the built game's <strong>Start scene</strong> in Project Settings; the open editor scene does not choose it for you.</li>
  <li>Double-click a Lua behaviour to edit it in your configured external code editor. Use <strong>Ctrl+S</strong> or <strong>File → Save all</strong> for authored documents and project settings, then <strong>Build → Build Project</strong> for the saved game.</li>
</ol>
<p>In the Inspector, changing an inherited default authors that field; <strong>Reset</strong> removes the authored value or instance override. Hover a property label to see its origin. <strong>Unpack preset</strong> turns preset-inherited components into editable independent components when you need to restructure them. An instance's <strong>Add Component</strong> affects only that instance; <strong>Open source prefab</strong> edits the shared source.</p>

<h2>Building a scene</h2>
<p>A <strong>scene</strong> is one file describing everything that loads together — a level, a menu screen, an arena. In the editor you assemble it like this:</p>
<p>Click an object in the 2D or 3D viewport to select it in the hierarchy and Inspector. Drag a selected object's translate, rotate, or scale gizmo to edit that object; crossing another object or leaving the viewport does not switch the drag target. Release to finish, or press <strong>Esc</strong> to cancel the edit.</p>
<p>Close-up picking includes the visible part of an object's bounds when the camera or near clipping plane is inside them. Fully clipped bounds are not picked. Transform handles keep a consistent screen size instead of growing across the viewport as you approach the object.</p>
<h2>Mesh surface controls</h2>
<figure class="docs-figure">
  <a href="/images/docs-mesh-surface-controls.png"><img src="/images/docs-mesh-surface-controls.png" alt="Native editor showing rough and metallic spheres, a transparent pane, and the Mesh Renderer metallic, roughness, opacity and surface-mode controls." width="5120" height="2806" loading="lazy"></a>
  <figcaption>Direct-lit rough and metallic surfaces, with a transparent pane selected. The metal has a direct-light highlight but no reflected environment.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Mesh Renderer exposes <strong>Metallic</strong>, <strong>Roughness</strong>, <strong>Opacity</strong> and <strong>Surface Mode</strong>. Metallic, roughness and opacity use 0&ndash;1 sliders. Omitted fields inherit the assigned material; without one, they use metallic 0, roughness 0.8, opacity 1 and opaque rendering. <strong>Reset</strong> removes the entity override rather than copying the current inherited value into the scene.</p>
<p>Choose <strong>Transparent</strong> and lower Opacity for tinted glass-like props. Opacity alone does not change an opaque surface into a blended one. Effective alpha multiplies texture alpha, colour alpha, material base-colour alpha, opacity and any fragment fade. Transparent meshes draw after opaque meshes, sorted back-to-front by renderer position, and do not write depth. Sorting intersecting geometry or individual triangles inside one transparent mesh remains approximate.</p>
<p>The built-in lit shader uses direct metallic/roughness lighting for directional, point and spot lights. Low roughness produces narrower highlights; metals use their base colour for specular light and have no diffuse lobe. There are no environment reflections, reflection probes, screen-space refraction or glass transmission in this path yet. A metal under ambient light alone can appear dark. Fully polished roughness values remain authored, but shading uses a small internal roughness floor to keep the specular calculation finite.</p>
<h2>Place props on terrain</h2>
<figure class="docs-figure">
  <a href="/images/docs-editor-prop-placement.png"><img src="/images/docs-editor-prop-placement.png" alt="Native editor with the Create 3D palette and a default light-grey cube on elevated terrain, without a texture asset." width="5120" height="2806" loading="lazy"></a>
  <figcaption>The Create 3D palette and an untextured unit cube on generated terrain. Click the capture for the full-size view.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The Hierarchy's <strong>Create 3D</strong> buttons create visible Cube, Sphere, Cylinder and Plane objects. Click to create near the current view centre, or drag a button into the Viewport to choose its position. A drop first intersects enabled generated terrain, including transformed terrain placements. Primitives are positioned with their base on the hit surface; prefab drops preserve their authored pivot and root offsets. If no terrain is hit, placement uses the ground plane within the current view's working range, then falls back along the cursor ray at the view focus distance.</p>
<p><strong>Add Empty</strong> creates a container, not a visible cube. The physics preset menu labels collision-only objects explicitly. Visible primitives use the built-in mesh and light-grey colour without requiring a texture or material. They have basic colliders but no Rigidbody: the cylinder uses a capsule approximation, and the plane uses a thin box. Adjust or remove those colliders to match your gameplay.</p>
<p>A <code>MeshRenderer</code> draws geometry. A <code>BoxCollider3D</code> describes collision, and a <code>Rigidbody3D</code> controls physical motion, gravity and mass. None replaces either of the others. Without a Rigidbody, a 3D collider is static. The Inspector explains physics-only entities and offers <strong>Add Mesh Renderer</strong> when a visible mesh is missing.</p>
<p>Move sensitivity follows the projected handle: when viewing an object from farther away, the same pixel drag moves it farther in world units. Scale additionally accounts for the object's dimensions and parent scale; rotation remains angular. Snapping still applies, and holding Shift bypasses it. Press <strong>F</strong> to frame a small or distant selected prop.</p>
<p>Placement is a one-time authored position, not a terrain attachment. Later terrain sculpting does not move independent houses or props automatically. Precise placement currently targets generated terrain, not arbitrary imported mesh surfaces.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Select the scene in the project sources so the scene stage is active.</li>
  <li>Add entities from the hierarchy panel. Each entity gets a <strong>stable ID</strong> that scripts, saves, and tests can rely on forever.</li>
  <li>Nest entities under their owner in the hierarchy. A weapon under a character's hand is a child; the tree mirrors the authored <code>children</code> array in JSON.</li>
  <li>Add components in the Inspector — a transform, a sprite or model, a collider and body, a script.</li>
  <li>Fill component fields. Omitted fields use canonical defaults; typed values become explicit in the document.</li>
  <li>Place prefab instances when a composition repeats across levels.</li>
  <li>Save. The editor patches the source file with a small diff.</li>
</ol>
<p>Why stable IDs? Because tests target nodes and entities by ID, saves refer to them, and network contracts name them. A stable ID is durable identity; a row number in a file is not.</p>

<h2>Setting the starting camera view</h2>
<p>Compose the desired view in the scene Viewport, select your game camera, then choose <strong>Scene → Align selected camera to view</strong>. This sets the camera's pose and projection in one undoable edit, including under a transformed parent or through a prefab instance override. Save, then use Game View to check framing at the game's aspect ratio. See <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'editor-workspace'}) }}">Workspace &amp; panels</a> for supported settings and camera-follow behavior.</p>

<h2>Authoring a terrain</h2>
<p>Use <strong>Assets → Create</strong> to start a Terrain Material, Terrain Material Set, or Terrain Palette. A material begins with a name and linear base colour. For a set, choose an existing terrain material for its initial <code>ground</code> role. For a palette, choose an existing <code>Model3D</code> asset for its initial <code>soil</code> role, or choose an entity prefab and a terrain material together. The editor offers references from the open project's registry and creates these as versioned data assets with stable <code>asset://</code> IDs and declared dependencies. Import the required art first when no reference is available.</p>
<p>Create a Terrain asset through <strong>Assets → Create → Terrain...</strong> and drag it into a 3D scene. Placing it selects the new terrain entity and opens its controls in the Inspector, ready for editing. Alternatively, add <strong>Terrain 3D</strong> to an entity with <code>Transform3D</code> and choose an existing terrain asset. Select that placement and choose <strong>Open Terrain Graph</strong> to configure size, cell resolution, seed, chunk size, landforms, biomes, rules, palette and connected modules. Generation settings are an <strong>unapplied draft</strong> until you generate; <strong>Discard draft</strong> restores the applied recipe.</p>
<p>Height-field generation runs in a background worker with progress and cancellation. The previous terrain remains visible until a completed result is validated and committed. Installing the result still assembles the generated meshes, so publication can take additional time for large terrains.</p>
<p>Choose a viewport brush in the Terrain component Inspector: <strong>Raise</strong>, <strong>Lower</strong>, <strong>Flatten</strong>, <strong>Smooth</strong>, <strong>Paint biome</strong>, <strong>Protect</strong>, or <strong>Paint exclusion</strong>. Adjust radius, strength and falloff; biome painting also chooses an existing biome ID. The brush ring follows the terrain surface, including its entity transform and parent hierarchy. Drag on the terrain and release to apply one undoable stroke. <strong>Esc</strong> cancels the stroke; <strong>Alt+drag</strong> navigates. Flatten uses the height at the start of the stroke.</p>
<p><strong>Protect</strong> fully captures every height sample inside its radius; it has no strength or falloff controls. Later seed and biome changes preserve that area. <strong>Clear protection</strong> removes all protection snapshots from the draft after confirmation; <strong>Clear all edits</strong> also removes sculpt edits and painted regions. Regenerate applies either removal, and Undo restores the applied recipe. Changing size or resolution with existing edits opens a decision dialog. You can keep radial edits at their existing local positions when the draft has no protection snapshots, clear all painted regions and edits, or cancel. Protection snapshots require their original grid.</p>
<p><strong>Apply changes to asset</strong> saves the shared terrain source and prepared surface, including painted regions, sculpt edits and protection snapshots. Edit directly in the scene Viewport; opening the isolated asset stage is optional. Other placements of that asset share its edits, while the scene keeps only its <code>Terrain3D.asset</code> reference and independent placement transforms. Explicit inline procedural recipes instead save to their owning scene or prefab. Generated mesh vertices do not enter authored source. Click a generated terrain chunk to select its authored owner. Return the brush to <strong>Select</strong> to use the ordinary transform tools.</p>

<h3>Composing terrain modules</h3>
<p>For a procedural chain, select the Terrain 3D entity and choose <strong>Open Terrain Graph</strong> in its Inspector. For a guided start, open the <strong>Terrain Presets</strong> tab beside Inspector and choose a starter landscape. Its graph includes editable comment nodes explaining each step. The independent <strong>Terrain Nodes</strong> panel lists the graph's current node types by category; drag one onto the graph canvas. Connect matching output and input pins, ending at a <strong>Terrain Output</strong> node. <strong>Starter graph</strong> creates a small working chain if you want a starting point. Graph edits remain an unapplied draft: inspect any node diagnostics, then press <strong>Generate</strong> to evaluate the graph and update the terrain. <strong>Undo graph</strong> and <strong>Redo graph</strong> change the draft before generation.</p>
<p>The graph builds the procedural base; authored biome regions, sculpt strokes, exclusions and protection remain separate surface layers. Regeneration replays those edits over the new base. A grid change with existing edits asks whether to keep compatible edits or clear them; protection snapshots require their original grid. See <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'terrain'}) }}">Terrain generation and editing</a> for the supported modules and their outputs.</p>

<h2>Building a HUD</h2>
<p>A <strong>HUD</strong> document is a tree of interface nodes: panels, labels, buttons, virtual sticks, and containers. The HUD stage is the same editor experience pointed at an interface tree instead of a world:</p>
<p>The HUD has its own document session, including when you edit the HUD attached to a scene. Its source, selection and Undo/Redo history stay separate from scene editing. You can keep HUD and Viewport visible together; clicking the scene does not replace the HUD displayed in its window. Clicking Inspector or UI Palette keeps the HUD editing context active.</p>
<p>While editing a HUD or UI prefab, the separate <strong>UI Palette</strong> panel lets you search or browse controls and the UI prefabs found in the project. Drag a card onto the HUD canvas. The drop uses the HUD's design coordinates and nests under the element at the pointer or the selected element. A drop is one undoable edit. In the 2D starter projects, reusable health bar and panel button row UI prefabs appear here; edit their prefab sources to change every instance. UI Palette is hidden outside HUD editing, and can be docked beside Inspector or elsewhere in the workspace.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Open the HUD source. The HUD stage shows the interface in the viewport at its design resolution.</li>
  <li>Compose nodes in the hierarchy. Layout conventions (omitted root, fill containers, <code>dock</code>, <code>stack</code>, <code>pad</code>, <code>at</code>) place nodes without hand-written anchors everywhere.</li>
  <li>Edit node fields in the Inspector: type defaults, style defaults, hex colors, actions for interactive controls.</li>
  <li>Keep readable and interactive content inside the safe area. Set <code>respect_safe_area: false</code> only for intentional edge-to-edge backgrounds, and keep controls clear of cutouts.</li>
  <li>Save — the same small-diff, atomic save as scenes.</li>
</ol>
<p>Interactive nodes (buttons, toggles, sliders, text inputs, virtual controls) connect to named input actions. Those actions are declared in the project file; see <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'input-bindings'}) }}">Input bindings</a> for how <code>virtual:</code> inputs fill them.</p>

<h2>Mutations are commands</h2>
<p>Every edit in the editor is a <strong>command</strong>: a named change with a known inverse. That design is what makes editing safe:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reversible</strong> — undo and redo walk the command history per document.</li>
  <li><strong>Validated</strong> — a command that would produce an invalid document is rejected before it lands, with a diagnostic in the diagnostics panel.</li>
  <li><strong>Keyed by stable IDs</strong> — commands target entities and nodes by ID, not by tree position. Rearranging the hierarchy does not break the undo history.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a change needs the preview to rebuild (for example, expanding a prefab instance) and the rebuild fails, the editor <strong>restores the document and the history</strong> to the last good state. You never keep a half-applied edit that the runtime cannot load.</p>

<h2>Editing prefab instances</h2>
<p>A <strong>prefab</strong> is a reusable template of entities. Scenes place it with an <strong>instance</strong> entry and a small <code>overrides</code> map instead of copying the whole tree into every level.</p>
<ul>
  <li>In the scene stage, an instance appears in the hierarchy as its expanded children, with overrides marked on the fields they set.</li>
  <li>Editing an overridden field records an override on the instance; editing a non-overridden field can be pushed back to the prefab document in the prefab stage.</li>
  <li>Override rules: absent fields inherit, objects merge, arrays replace, <code>null</code> removes, and a dotted key sets one nested field (for example <code>"body.Transform2D.position"</code>).</li>
</ul>
<p>The prefab stage edits the template itself, with the same Inspector, undo, and save behavior.</p>

<h2>How saves keep diffs small</h2>
<p>Editor saves must look good in review. The rules:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Save <strong>patches source with small diffs</strong> — only the values you changed are rewritten.</li>
  <li><strong>Float noise is normalized</strong> so <code>0.30000000000000004</code> does not pollute the file; neighboring multiline style and key order stay stable.</li>
  <li>Saves are <strong>atomic and conflict-aware</strong>: the write either completes fully or leaves the previous file intact, and an external change to the file on disk is detected instead of silently overwritten.</li>
</ul>
<p>Human-authored source stays human-readable. Generated content never becomes the authored identity.</p>

<h2>Recovery</h2>
<p>If the editor or the machine stops unexpectedly, the recovery path restores unsaved document state the next time the workspace opens the project. Recovery data is per-user, like dock layout, and never ships inside a package or a build artifact.</p>

